[PATCH] sf2000: default SF2000 volume to the stock level (90 -> 0xcb)

The stock SF2000 device tree runs the i2so platform at volume 90, which
maps to 0xcb on the vendor volume curve - the same byte the bootloader
writes.  The previous patch set SF2000 to UniFrog runtime value 65 (0x61),
which is quieter than the reference firmware; restore the stock level and
keep UniFrog value 75 (0x8f) for GB300.

Signed-off-by: SF2000 Linux project <noreply@example.com>
---
diff --git a/sound/drivers/sf2000-pcm.c b/sound/drivers/sf2000-pcm.c
index 1111111..2222222 100644
@@ -65,14 +65,16 @@
 #define SF2000_SYS_I2S_DIV_CTL2	0x474
 #define SF2000_I2S_DIV_CTL_BITS	(BIT(16) | BIT(21))
 /*
- * Default SND0+0x92 FADE volume bytes, taken from the vendor libauddrv
- * volume curve (extracted from the UniFrog firmware).  The vendor maps a
- * 0-100 user volume through a non-linear table onto this byte, 100 -> 0xff;
- * UniFrog's defaults are 65 on SF2000 (-> 0x61) and 75 on GB300 (-> 0x8f).
- * Writing the raw maximum (0xff) with full-scale game samples overdrives
- * the small analog speaker: audible clipping, harsh treble and crackle.
+ * Default SND0+0x92 FADE volume bytes.  The vendor libauddrv maps a 0-100
+ * volume through a non-linear table onto this byte (100 -> 0xff); the stock
+ * SF2000 device tree ships the i2so platform at volume 90, which lands at
+ * 0xcb on that curve - the same byte the bootloader uses.  UniFrog lowers
+ * it to 65 (0x61) on SF2000 and 75 (0x8f) on GB300 at runtime.  The raw
+ * maximum (0xff) with full-scale game samples overdrives the small analog
+ * speaker: audible clipping, harsh treble and crackle; match the stock
+ * default instead.
  */
-#define SF2000_VOLUME_SF2000	0x61
+#define SF2000_VOLUME_SF2000	0xcb
 #define SF2000_VOLUME_GB300	0x8f
 #define SF2000_RATE_MIN		8000
 #define SF2000_RATE_MAX		48000
